---
import Product from '../../layouts/Product.astro';
import CaseworkFlow from '../../components/CaseworkFlow.astro';
import { products } from '../../data/products';
const product = products.find((p) => p.key === 'casework')!;
---
<Product
  product={product}
  heading="Staff review requests and record decisions in Registry Casework."
  description="Your teams need to assign review requests and see who is responsible. They need to see when a decision is due and record the outcome. Registry Casework manages that work. It returns the decision to the service that asked for the review."
>
  <section id="product-example" class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="casework-example-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Example: a licence correction</p>
        <h2 id="casework-example-title">Assign the request, track its due date and record the decision.</h2>
        <p>A licence holder asks to correct the activities listed in the professional register. The licensing application sends the request to Registry Casework for staff review. Staff check the supporting information and record their decision.</p>
      </div>
      <CaseworkFlow />
      <p class="product-note">An approval records the staff decision. Permission to change a register stays under that register’s rules.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="casework-inbox-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Practical capabilities</p>
        <h2 id="casework-inbox-title">See who is responsible, what is due and what was decided.</h2>
        <p>Review requests can come from:</p>
        <ul class="prose-list">
          <li>a register</li>
          <li>a case system</li>
          <li>another service</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Registry Casework places each request in the agreed queue. It records the request’s progress until staff reach a decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="product-facts">
        <article><h3>Assign and reassign work</h3><p>You send each kind of request to the right queue. An officer takes responsibility for a request. They can release it if they cannot continue. Supervisors can reassign work when staffing changes.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Track due dates</h3><p>You set the time allowed for each review step. You also set the working-day calendar. Staff can see when a request is due. Registry Casework identifies when a follow-up is due. It does not send messages. A connected service sends any email or SMS notice.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Record the decision</h3><p>Staff can record an approval, a refusal or a request for changes. They can also record another outcome that you have configured. The service that asked for the review receives the result. That service takes the next step under its own rules.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="fit" class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="casework-fit-title">
    <div class="section-inner product-fit">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it fits your institution</p>
        <h2 id="casework-fit-title">Connect staff review to your service.</h2>
        <p>Registry Casework manages staff reviews. An implementation provides or adapts the staff screens. It also connects sign-in and the service that requests reviews.</p>
        <p>Registry Casework can accept review requests on its own. It can also connect to proposed changes in <a href="/products/base-registry/">Base Registry Engine</a>, which keeps a register’s records. Connecting another register needs additional integration work.</p>
      </div>
      <dl>
        <dt>You define the review</dt><dd>You decide which requests need staff review and which team receives each kind. You also decide which outcomes are allowed and when the decision is due.</dd>
        <dt>You choose who delivers it</dt><dd>Your own team or another delivery partner can deliver the implementation. Or we can deliver it for you. The work covers providing or adapting the staff screens. It also covers connecting sign-in and the service that requests reviews, and testing the whole process. Your own team or a provider you appoint can operate Registry Casework. Or we can host and run it for you. We quote our hosting and operation separately.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="developers" class="section" aria-labelledby="casework-developers-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">For developers and implementers</p>
        <h2 id="casework-developers-title">Prepare a proposed change for review.</h2>
        <p>The introductory tutorial prepares and validates matching configuration for Base Registry Engine and Registry Casework. It does not run a review. It does not apply a change.</p>
        <p>To implement Registry Casework, read the <a href="https://docs.registrystack.org/configure/casework/">configuration guide</a> and the <a href="https://docs.registrystack.org/operate/casework/">operations documentation</a>.</p>
        <p><a class="inline-cta" href={product.tutorialHref}>{product.tutorialLabel} →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Product>
